   {
    "chapter": "Week 1",
    "text": "The cell membrane is a phospholipid bilayer: two sheets of fat-like molecules with their water-loving heads facing out and water-fearing tails facing in. That arrangement makes the membrane selectively permeable \u2014 small uncharged molecules slip through, while ions and large molecules need help from embedded proteins. Channel proteins form pores, carrier proteins shuttle specific cargo, and receptor proteins pass signals inward. Cholesterol tucked between the phospholipids keeps the membrane fluid but stable. The membrane is therefore both a wall and a customs post: it decides what enters, what leaves, and what the cell hears about the world outside.\n\nCheck yourself: What does the cell membrane do? Controls what enters and leaves the cell.",
