   {
    "chapter": "Week 2",
    "text": "Photosynthesis is how plants, algae, and some bacteria turn light energy into stored chemical energy. Inside chloroplasts, pigments \u2014 chiefly chlorophyll \u2014 absorb mostly blue and red light, which is why leaves look green. In the light-dependent reactions, water is split, releasing oxygen, and its energy is captured in the carriers ATP and NADPH. The Calvin cycle then spends those carriers to build sugars from carbon dioxide, no light required. The balance sheet: carbon dioxide plus water becomes glucose plus oxygen \u2014 and nearly every food chain on Earth runs on that captured sunlight.",
