   {
    "chapter": "Week 2",
    "text": "Cellular respiration releases the energy photosynthesis stored. It runs in three acts. Glycolysis, in the cytoplasm, splits one glucose into two pyruvate molecules and nets a small payment of ATP \u2014 no oxygen needed. The Krebs cycle, in the mitochondrial matrix, strips the pyruvate down to carbon dioxide and loads energy carriers. The electron transport chain then spends those carriers, with oxygen as the final electron acceptor, forming water and driving the bulk of ATP production. Total yield: roughly thirty ATP per glucose \u2014 the cell's spendable currency, earned by controlled burning instead of fire.",
