   {
    "chapter": "Week 3",
    "text": "Before a cell can divide, it must copy its DNA \u2014 all of it, once, accurately. The double helix unzips as helicase breaks the hydrogen bonds between base pairs, and each exposed strand becomes a template. DNA polymerase builds the new strand by matching free nucleotides to the template \u2014 A with T, C with G \u2014 but it can only build in one direction, so one strand is made continuously and the other in short Okazaki fragments that ligase later seals. The result is semi-conservative: each daughter molecule keeps one original strand and one new one, and proofreading keeps errors near one in a billion bases.",
