   {
    "chapter": "Week 3",
    "text": "Mitosis divides one nucleus into two genetically identical nuclei. In prophase the copied chromosomes condense and the spindle \u2014 a scaffold of microtubules \u2014 begins to form. Metaphase lines the chromosomes along the cell's equator, each attached to spindle fibers from both poles, a checkpoint that ensures every daughter gets a full set. Anaphase pulls the sister chromatids apart to opposite poles. Telophase wraps each set in a new nuclear envelope, and cytokinesis then splits the cytoplasm. One cell in, two identical cells out \u2014 the mechanism behind growth, repair, and asexual reproduction.",
